  "body": "## What's New in v1.15.0\r\n\r\nYou can now install OpenSpec without Node.js, using one command, and `openspec update` can now show what it would change before changing anything.\r\n\r\n### New\r\n\r\n- **Standalone install** - Install OpenSpec with one command and no Node.js: `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec/main/scripts/binary/install.sh | sh` on macOS and Linux, or `irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec/main/scripts/binary/install.ps1 | iex` on Windows. Every release now ships binaries for macOS, Linux (glibc and musl) and Windows on x64 and arm64, with `SHA256SUMS`. npm and the other package managers keep working as before.\r\n- **`openspec update --dry-run`** - Reports the files an update would add, change or delete without writing anything; add `--json` for machine-readable output.\r\n\r\n### Improved\r\n\r\n- **Faster `--json` commands** - `status`, `instructions`, `new change`, `templates`, `validate` and `schema` no longer load the spinner package when run with `--json`, so they start a little faster. Output is unchanged.\r\n- **`openspec feedback`** - Filed issues now match the bug form, and the manual-submission link opens the bug form with your message, version and environment filled in.\r\n\r\n### Fixed\r\n\r\n- **`openspec new change`** - The progress line now names the schema the change is actually created with, instead of always saying `spec-driven`.\r\n- **`/opsx:verify`** - A REMOVED requirement is matched to its baseline by its exact name, so a similarly named replacement is no longer mistaken for it.\r\n\r\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec/compare/v1.14.3...v1.15.0\r\n",
